  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough Monday...\n\nA hot afternoon with highs in the mid to upper 80s, possibly a few\n90s today. Combination of unseasonable heat, very dry airmass, and\nbreezy to gusty winds will keep critical (west) to elevated\n(elsewhere) fire conditions in place through early this evening. A\nRed Flag Warning is in place for the western foothills (Livingston\nto Big Timber and north to Harlowton) until 8pm this evening. \nPlease use extra caution to prevent wildfires today. \n\nWarm, dry, breezy conditions will continue through the work week.\nA weak disturbance well to the north of the state will tilt the  \nflow aloft more northwesterly on Tuesday, lowering heights and\nallowing winds to turn northerly and pull slightly cooler air in\nfor Tuesday. This will lower the fire weather concerns for\nTuesday, so do not anticipate further fire weather highlites\nbeyond today. Highs Tuesday should be 5 to 10 degrees cooler than\ntoday. \n\nTemperatures continue to trend cooler by a few degrees on\nWednesday with easterly winds over the area. Temperatures hold in\nthe 70s Thursday with winds turning southerly and then\nsouthwesterly through the day. NBM might be a bit cool for\nThursday highs as western ridging is building into the area and\nwould expect breezy downslope winds during the afternoon hours.\nSo, could be 4 or 5 degrees warmer than forecast, which is\ncaptured in the NBM 75th percentile for Thursdays highs. \n\nMonitoring Friday as a potential critical fire weather day.\nStronger downslope winds developing ahead of potential weekend \nstorm system have the potential to push temperatures back to\nlevels similar to todays forecast, with stronger gusts. Given a\nweek of warm dry conditions, fuels will likely be more critical by\nthe end of this week compared to today as well. As with Thursday,\nthe deterministic NBM may be on the cool side for Friday. All of\nthis depends on the character and track of the potential synoptic\nstorm system for this weekend.\n\nSaturday is a transition day. 28% of the ensemble members \n(Can/EC/GFS) are advertising a trof (cool/wetter) over the area\nSaturday, 40% are showing SW flow pattern (warm/showers), and 32%\nshow ridge or flat flow (warm, breezy, dry). So, precipitation\nchances will be developing for Saturday, but not a slam dunk, and\ntemperatures are leaning toward remaining warm with deterministic\nNBM showing mainly 70s, with a few 80s over southeast areas. \n\nSunday/Monday are showing a trend toward a trof either over the\narea (cool/wet/mtn snow) or in the vicinity/just west (not as cool\nbut unsettled to wet) with 85-90% of ensembles clustered into\nthose categories. That still leaves 10-15% of solutions showing\nridging aloft with continued warm/dry conditions, which while an\noutlier is still double digit probability. \n\nA lot of discussion in social media about some outlier snow\nsolutions for the lower elevations. Currently there are about 30%\nof ensembles showing accumulating snow in the lower elevations\nlate weekend into early next week, and around 5 percent showing\nvery large accumulations. That still leaves 70% showing little to\nno lower elevation snowfall. Keep in mind that these solutions are\nstill a week or more out in the future during the Fall when the\natmosphere is in flux and models can struggle. So take any big\nsnowfall deterministic forecasts with a big grain of salt. That\nsaid the models are in good agreement with a pattern change this\nweekend to more unsettled fall weather. Take this into \nconsideration with weekend plans especially in the high country\nwhere snowfall will be more likely. Chambers",
